const PS2_PASSAGE_1 = `Henri Tajfel and John Turner's Social Identity Theory (SIT) proposes that individuals derive part of their self-concept from membership in social groups. The theory consists of three processes: (1) Social categorization — people categorize themselves and others into social groups (in-groups and out-groups). (2) Social identification — individuals adopt the identity of the group they categorize themselves as belonging to. (3) Social comparison — people compare their in-group favorably to out-groups to maintain positive self-esteem.

Researchers tested SIT in a classic minimal group paradigm experiment. Participants were randomly assigned to groups ("blue team" or "red team") ostensibly based on an aesthetic preference task. Despite the arbitrary assignment, participants consistently allocated more resources to members of their own group than to out-group members, even when doing so was economically irrational. The mere assignment to a group was sufficient to produce in-group favoritism.

Tajfel also described ethnocentrism — the tendency to view one's own group as superior — as a near-universal feature of intergroup perception. He distinguished prejudice (a negative attitude toward a group) from discrimination (differential treatment of group members). Stereotypes — overgeneralized beliefs about group characteristics — can be automatic (implicit) or deliberate (explicit) and can lead to self-fulfilling prophecies when members of stereotyped groups internalize group expectations.`

const PS2_PASSAGE_2 = `Attribution theory examines how individuals explain the causes of events and behaviors. Fritz Heider distinguished between internal (dispositional) attributions — explaining behavior by referring to the actor's character, personality, or intentions — and external (situational) attributions — explaining behavior by reference to situational factors outside the actor's control.

Lee Ross identified the fundamental attribution error (FAE): the tendency to over-attribute others' behavior to dispositional factors and under-attribute situational factors. For example, observers typically conclude that a person who acts rudely has a rude personality, even when that person is under situational constraints that would make anyone behave rudely.

Subsequent research identified actor-observer bias: actors tend to explain their own behavior in terms of situational factors, while observers attribute the same behavior to the actor's disposition. Self-serving bias refers to the tendency to attribute successes to internal factors and failures to external factors, protecting self-esteem. Harold Kelley proposed the covariation model of attribution, in which people consider consensus (do others behave the same way?), distinctiveness (does the actor behave this way only in this situation?), and consistency (does the actor behave this way consistently over time?) to determine whether behavior is caused by the person, the stimulus, or the circumstances.`

const PS2_PASSAGE_3 = `Learning theorists have proposed two major forms of associative learning. Classical conditioning (Pavlovian conditioning) involves learning that a neutral stimulus (conditioned stimulus, CS) predicts a biologically significant event (unconditioned stimulus, US), producing a conditioned response (CR) similar to the unconditioned response (UR). Ivan Pavlov demonstrated this with dogs: a bell (CS) paired with food (US) eventually elicited salivation (CR) without food. Key phenomena include acquisition (CR development), extinction (CS presented without US → CR decreases), spontaneous recovery (return of CR after extinction rest), stimulus generalization (CR to stimuli similar to CS), and stimulus discrimination (CR to CS but not to similar stimuli).

Operant conditioning (Skinnerian or instrumental conditioning) describes how behavior is modified by its consequences. B.F. Skinner showed that organisms learn to produce behaviors (operants) that are followed by reinforcement. Positive reinforcement adds a pleasant stimulus to increase behavior; negative reinforcement removes an aversive stimulus to increase behavior. Positive punishment adds an aversive stimulus to decrease behavior; negative punishment removes a pleasant stimulus to decrease behavior. Schedules of reinforcement (fixed ratio, variable ratio, fixed interval, variable interval) affect both rate of learning and resistance to extinction. Variable ratio schedules produce the highest response rates and greatest resistance to extinction.`

